<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!DOCTYPE flight_plan SYSTEM "../flight_plan.dtd">
<!--
  IMAV Mission 2, VALKENBURG REPLICA of mission2_strasbourg.xml - thermal
  hot-spot survey of a stand-in fire zone.

  Same survey, same code, same variables; only the site changes: origin
  and ground_alt, the FZ1-FZ7 flyzone and its buffered geofence, the
  launch and landing waypoints (mission4.xml's Valkenburg set) and a
  stand-in fire zone. Keep the two files in step - a change to the survey
  logic belongs in both. Where the notes below talk about Strasbourg (the
  hexagon, the placeholder box), they describe the original site.

  THE TASK
    Two hot spots somewhere in the red "Fire zone" on the site map. Report
    their GPS coordinates in decimal degrees to 5 m accuracy, no later than
    5 MINUTES AFTER LANDING. Single aircraft, flight is timed, energy
    consumption is measured, fixed-wing favoured.

  THE ONE RULE THAT SHAPES EVERYTHING
    Coordinates are due five minutes after landing, NOT in flight. So the
    aircraft does not have to detect anything. Its entire job is to
    guarantee coverage of the fire zone and produce frames that are
    well geotagged. Detection and georeferencing run on a laptop after
    touchdown. That is a far easier and far more debuggable problem than
    onboard detection, and it costs nothing in score.

    Consequence: this flight plan contains no detection logic at all, and
    should not acquire any. It is a survey plan.

  THE CAMERA
    LWIR, 256 x 192, 12 um pitch, 4.3 mm f/1.2, IFOV 2.79 mrad, 40 x 30 deg.
    Verified self-consistent: 256 x 2.79 mrad = 40.9 deg, and 2.79 mrad x
    4.3 mm = 12.0 um. The alternative 25 x 19 deg option implies a ~7 mm lens.

    TAKE THE 40 x 30 LENS. The narrow one buys nothing. At whatever altitude
    gives N pixels on the target the swath comes out the same either way -
    h scales as 1/IFOV and swath as h*IFOV, so they cancel and only the 256
    pixel count sets it (0.3 m target at 3 px: 36.7 m / 26.7 m swath with the
    wide lens, 58.7 m / 26.0 m with the narrow). The narrow lens just forces
    you higher, which makes the georeferencing attitude term worse - 1.02 m
    against 0.64 m at 1 deg of attitude error.

  WHY 40 m AGL
    An earlier revision of this file said 80 m, worked through against a
    1280 x 1024 sensor. That was correct arithmetic on the wrong camera. At
    256 x 192 the ground sample distance is FOUR TIMES coarser:

        AGL    GSD     swath    0.3 m target   0.6 m target
         40   11.2cm   29.1 m      2.7 px         5.4 px
         60   16.7cm   43.7 m      1.8 px         3.6 px
         80   22.3cm   58.2 m      1.3 px         2.7 px

    At 80 m a 1 foot plate is 1.3 pixels - a single warm pixel, not a
    detection. Maximum altitude is now set by target size alone: 35.8 m for
    3 px on a 0.3 m target, 71.7 m for 3 px on a 0.6 m one.

    40 m is the hedge between those: 2.7 px on the small case, 5.4 px on the
    large. Slightly under the usual 3 px rule for 0.3 m targets, but that
    rule assumes a cluttered scene, and the mock imagery from this camera
    shows a near-saturated blob against COLD vegetation - the confusers are
    at the opposite end of the histogram from the target, which is a far
    easier discrimination than the rule is sized for.

    >>> TARGET SIZE IS THE CRITICAL UNKNOWN. 0.3 m versus 0.6 m is the
    difference between 36 m and 72 m, and between 12 lines and 6. If the
    organisers will say what the hot spots physically are, that single fact
    is worth more than any other tuning in this file. <<<

  WHY NOT HIGHER (the energy argument, and why it loses)
    Altitude is nearly free for georeferencing accuracy and pays for itself
    in battery - see the measured numbers below. That argument still holds in
    isolation, and it is why an earlier revision chose 80 m. It is now
    OVERRIDDEN by detection: at 80 m this survey would be short, efficient,
    well georeferenced, and blind. For the 130 x 150 m zone at 50% sidelap:

        AGL   lines   time    0.3 m target
         40      9    175 s      2.7 px
         60      6    114 s      1.8 px
         80      5     94 s      1.3 px

    Flying low roughly doubles the survey. Pay it.

  THE OLD ALTITUDE REASONING, kept because it still governs everything else:

      1. Georeferencing error = sqrt(GPS^2 + (h*attitude)^2 + (v*sync)^2).
         Only the attitude term scales with height, and it is RANDOM, so it
         averages away over the many frames that see each spot. After
         averaging, the total is dominated by SYSTEMATIC terms - GPS bias
         (~2 m) and residual boresight (~0.5 m) - which do not depend on
         altitude at all. Measured on the projection: 80 m and 120 m differ
         by 6 cm at 10 sightings. Altitude is almost free for accuracy.

      2. Pixels on target. This is what stops us going higher, and with a
         256 px sensor it binds far lower than it would with 1280 - see the
         table above.

      3. Flight time and energy are both scored, and both improve with
         altitude (wider swath, fewer lines).

    IS CLIMBING WORTH THE BATTERY? YES, decisively - measured, not assumed.
    From this airframe's own SITL power telemetry: 19.1 W in level cruise at
    12 m/s (17% throttle), 26.2 W climbing at 2 m/s. Over a 400x400 m area:

        80 m AGL: 3364 m of track, 280 s -> 1.48 Wh cruise + 0.52 Wh climb
        40 m AGL: 6328 m of track, 527 s -> 2.79 Wh cruise + 0.26 Wh climb

    Flying at 80 m instead of 40 m SAVES 1.05 Wh, a third of the total. The
    extra 40 m of climb costs 0.26 Wh; halving the survey saves 1.31 Wh. The
    climb is repaid roughly five times over, and some of it comes back again
    as a power-off glide on the descent to land. Height is bought once and
    spent across every line, which is why it is nearly always worth it for a
    survey - the argument only reverses for an area so small that the climb
    is a large fraction of the whole flight.

    So: go as high as pixels-on-target allows. With this camera that is
    about 40 m, far below the competition's 80 m AGL cap, so the cap is no
    longer the binding constraint - the sensor is.

  WHY DISTANCE-BASED CAMERA TRIGGERING
    dc_distance() shoots every survey_shot_interval METRES of ground track,
    not every N seconds. With wind, ground speed varies along and across the
    lines; a time-based trigger would bunch frames on the downwind legs and
    thin them on the upwind ones. Distance-based gives uniform forward
    overlap regardless, which is what both the mosaic and the multi-frame
    averaging want.

    NOTE it is dc_distance(), not the confusingly named dc_Survey() - see
    the Start Survey block for why the latter is wrong for a lawnmower.

  WHAT ACTUALLY DECIDES THE SCORE (and it is not this file)
    After multi-frame averaging the error budget is essentially
    sqrt(2.0^2 + 0.5^2) = 2.06 m of pure systematic error, so even with a
    perfect flight the 95% figure is about 4.1 m against a 5 m requirement.
    Two things move that, neither of which lives in a flight plan:

      RTK or PPK removes the 2 m GPS term outright. Biggest single win
      available and it is a procurement decision, not a code change.

      BORESIGHT CALIBRATION. The camera is never perfectly square to the
      airframe, and 2 deg of mounting error is 2.79 m at 80 m - larger than
      everything else combined. Unlike attitude noise it is a FIXED bias and
      averaging over frames does not reduce it at all. Calibrate by flying
      over a surveyed point and solving for the roll/pitch offsets.

    Then validate end to end: put a hot target on a GPS-measured spot, fly
    this survey, and check what the pipeline returns. An error budget on
    paper is not evidence.

  LWIR NOTES THAT AFFECT THIS PLAN
    FFC (the shutter flat-field correction, every 30-120 s) blanks the image
    for 0.5-2 s. THIS GOT TIGHTER when the altitude dropped: the along-track
    footprint is only 21.4 m at 40 m, so a ground point is in view for just
    1.8 s at 12 m/s, and a 2 s blank CAN swallow it. At 80 m the dwell was
    5-6 s and this was a non-issue.

    What saves it is the 50% sidelap: nearly every point is imaged from two
    adjacent lines, flown a minute or more apart, so a shutter event would
    have to land on the same ground twice to open a real hole. Over a 175 s
    survey with FFC every 60 s that is about 3 events, roughly 5% of the area
    blanked once and almost none of it blanked twice.

    LEAVE FFC ENABLED. Disabling it to avoid gaps would trade a covered risk
    for real radiometric drift. If the core allows it, force a manual FFC
    just before Start Survey and set the automatic period as long as
    possible - that pushes the events out of the survey window entirely.

    Motion smear is 1-3 px at a microbolometer's 8-20 ms thermal time
    constant and 12 m/s. Acceptable, and symmetric along track so it does
    not bias the centroid.

    Frames taken IN THE TURNS should be discarded in post-processing, not
    prevented here: attitude error in a bank is far worse than in level
    flight and sits at the top of the error budget. Filtering on |phi| in
    the log is simpler and loses nothing, so the camera runs continuously.

  >>> BEFORE FLYING - BOTH POLYGONS BELOW ARE PLACEHOLDERS <<<
    F1..F4 (fire zone) and FZ_A..FZ_D (flight zone) are sketched from the
    site map's dimensions (flight zone about 360 m across the top, 440 m
    down the west side) and are NOT surveyed coordinates. Replace them from
    the organisers' KML, or drag them in the pprzgcs Flight Plan Editor.
    The survey adapts to whatever polygon F1..F4 describes; nothing else
    needs changing when they move.
-->
<flight_plan alt="50" ground_alt="0" lat0="52.1681239" lon0="4.4124784" max_dist_from_home="1500" name="Mission2_Valkenburg" security_height="5" geofence_max_alt="85">
  <header>
#include "autopilot.h"
#include "firmwares/fixedwing/guidance/energy_ctrl.h"
#include "modules/digital_cam/dc.h"
#include "modules/nav/nav_survey_poly_osam.h"
#include "modules/nav/nav_mission4_geofence.h"

/* Shoot ONLY while the wings are near level, so frames come from the
   straight legs and not the turns.

   The camera is bolted to the airframe, so in a turn the footprint swings
   off to the side AND the attitude estimate is at its worst: ahrs_float_dcm
   has no external attitude aid, and its accelerometer-based levelling is
   corrupted by centripetal acceleration exactly when banked. Turn frames
   are therefore the ones that would georeference worst, and they cover
   ground the straight legs already cover - pure liability.

   Gating on ROLL rather than the survey module's own state because
   nav_survey_poly_osam keeps CSurveyStatus (Init/Entry/Sweep/Turn)
   file-static and does not export it. Roll is the better test anyway: it
   also suppresses the roll-out transient at the start of a leg, where the
   aircraft is still settling onto the line and cross-track error is largest.

   Sets dc_autoshoot DIRECTLY rather than calling dc_stop()/dc_distance():
   dc_stop() also fires a DC_INFO downlink on every call, which at 20 Hz
   would flood the link, and dc_distance() resets last_shot_pos, which would
   restart the along-track spacing at every gate transition. Toggling the
   mode preserves both the interval and the last shot position.

   No coverage is lost: the turns happen OUTSIDE the polygon, so every
   suppressed frame is of ground the survey lines cover anyway. Measured on
   the Talon without this gate: 2115 frames at a mean roll of 19.2 deg, 1776
   of them past 10 deg. With it: 351 frames, mean 4.7 deg, max 19.1 deg,
   against a 46.8 deg peak bank in the turns. */
/* Lay the survey lines PARALLEL TO THE LONGEST EDGE of the search polygon.

   Two reasons, and the second is the expensive one:

     Parallel to a real boundary. Lines that cut across the zone at an
     arbitrary angle leave ragged part-width strips along the edges, which
     is where a hot spot is most likely to be missed.

     Fewest turns. Every line change costs a turn, and on this aircraft a
     turn is enormous relative to the line spacing - POLY_OSAM_MIN_RADIUS is
     30 m, so 60 m of lateral travel is needed to swap lines while the swath
     at 40 m is only 29 m. Running the lines along the LONG axis gives the
     fewest, longest legs and so the fewest of those turns.

   The obvious call, nav_survey_poly_osam_setup_towards(F1, 4, sweep, F2),
   hardcodes the F1-F2 edge. That happens to be the long edge of the current
   placeholder, but the real fire zone is a rotated rectangle and there is
   no guarantee its corners arrive in an order that puts the long side
   first - if F1-F2 came out as the SHORT side you would silently get twice
   the lines and twice the turns. Choosing the longest edge is orientation-
   and order-independent, so it survives whatever the organisers' KML gives.

   Orientation convention mirrors the module's own setup_towards: a LINE
   angle from atan(dy/dx), degrees CCW from east, in (-90, +90). Direction
   sense does not matter for a sweep, only the line direction. */
/* Push the survey lines OUT PAST the search polygon along the sweep axis,
   so every turn and every roll-out happens outside the hot-spot area and
   the aircraft crosses the real boundary already straight and level.

   Why this is needed even though the U-turns are already outside: a turn
   does not end where the curve ends. The aircraft rolls out over some
   distance, and a gentle roll-out is still a curve - at 12 m/s a 10 deg
   bank is a 83 m radius, which the roll gate happily lets through, so
   frames were being taken on visibly curved track just inside the edge.

   Only the two edges PERPENDICULAR to the sweep are moved, by `margin`
   each. That lengthens every line but does NOT add lines, so the cost is
   2*margin per line rather than a whole extra pass - expanding the polygon
   uniformly would widen it too and add lines for nothing.

   The expanded corners go into scratch waypoints so F1..F4 stay the true
   fire zone for the GCS and for any post-flight coverage check. */
static inline void mission2_expand_along_sweep(uint8_t src_wp, uint8_t n, uint8_t dst_wp, float margin)
{
  uint8_t i;
  float best = -1.0f, ux = 1.0f, uy = 0.0f;
  float cx = 0.0f, cy = 0.0f;

  for (i = 0; i != n; i++) {
    cx += waypoints[src_wp + i].x;
    cy += waypoints[src_wp + i].y;
  }
  cx /= (float)n;
  cy /= (float)n;

  /* Same longest-edge rule the sweep orientation uses, so the expansion is
     along the direction the lines will actually run. */
  for (i = 0; i != n; i++) {
    uint8_t a = src_wp + i;
    uint8_t b = src_wp + ((i + 1) % n);
    float dx = waypoints[b].x - waypoints[a].x;
    float dy = waypoints[b].y - waypoints[a].y;
    float d2 = dx * dx + dy * dy;
    if (!signbit(d2 - best)) { best = d2; ux = dx; uy = dy; }
  }
  float un = sqrtf(ux * ux + uy * uy);
  if (signbit(un - 0.001f)) { un = 1.0f; }
  ux /= un;
  uy /= un;

  for (i = 0; i != n; i++) {
    float x = waypoints[src_wp + i].x;
    float y = waypoints[src_wp + i].y;
    float s = (x - cx) * ux + (y - cy) * uy;   /* along-sweep position */
    float k = margin;
    if (signbit(s)) { k = -margin; }           /* push each end outward */
    waypoints[dst_wp + i].x = x + ux * k;
    waypoints[dst_wp + i].y = y + uy * k;
    waypoints[dst_wp + i].a = waypoints[src_wp + i].a;
  }
}

/* tan(HFOV / 2) for the 40 x 30 deg LWIR lens: swath = 2 * height * this */
#define MISSION2_TAN_HALF_HFOV 0.36397f

/* The NUMBER OF LINES follows the polygon: the width across the lines is
   split into the fewest equal strips no wider than max_sweep, and that
   exact spacing goes to the module. The module puts the first line half a
   spacing in from the edge, so N lines land at (k + 0.5) * width / N and
   the outer lines sit exactly half a spacing from each edge - full
   coverage for any zone, with no ragged last strip and no half-sweep. */
static inline void mission2_survey_along_longest_edge(uint8_t first_wp, uint8_t n, float max_sweep)
{
  uint8_t i;
  float best = -1.0f;
  float bdx = 1.0f, bdy = 0.0f;

  for (i = 0; i != n; i++) {
    uint8_t a = first_wp + i;
    uint8_t b = first_wp + ((i + 1) % n);
    float dx = waypoints[b].x - waypoints[a].x;
    float dy = waypoints[b].y - waypoints[a].y;
    float d2 = dx * dx + dy * dy;
    if (!signbit(d2 - best)) { best = d2; bdx = dx; bdy = dy; }
  }
  /* Width across the lines: spread of the corners along the edge normal. */
  float len = sqrtf(best);
  if (signbit(len - 0.001f)) { len = 1.0f; }
  float nx = -bdy / len, ny = bdx / len;
  float lo = 0.0f, hi = 0.0f;
  for (i = 0; i != n; i++) {
    float t = waypoints[first_wp + i].x * nx + waypoints[first_wp + i].y * ny;
    if (i == 0) { lo = t; hi = t; }
    if (signbit(t - lo)) { lo = t; }
    if (!signbit(t - hi)) { hi = t; }
  }
  float width = hi - lo;
  if (signbit(max_sweep - 1.0f)) { max_sweep = 1.0f; }
  /* -0.001: a width of exactly k strips must give k lines, not k+1 */
  float lines = ceilf(width / max_sweep - 0.001f);
  if (signbit(lines - 1.0f)) { lines = 1.0f; }
  float sweep = width / lines;

  /* atan(dy/dx) blows up on a due-north edge; nudge dx the way the module
     does rather than switching to atan2, so the convention stays identical. */
  if (signbit(fabs(bdx) - 0.000001f)) { bdx = 0.000001f; }
  nav_survey_poly_osam_setup(first_wp, n, sweep, DegOfRad(atan(bdy / bdx)));
}

/* True if (x, y) is at least `margin` metres inside the convex polygon
   waypoints[first_wp .. first_wp+n-1]. The winding is taken from the signed
   area, so a relabelled or re-dragged zone needs no code change. */
static inline bool mission2_inside_zone(uint8_t first_wp, uint8_t n, float x, float y, float margin)
{
  uint8_t i;
  float area2 = 0.0f;
  for (i = 0; i != n; i++) {
    uint8_t a = first_wp + i;
    uint8_t b = first_wp + ((i + 1) % n);
    area2 += waypoints[a].x * waypoints[b].y - waypoints[b].x * waypoints[a].y;
  }
  float wind = 1.0f;
  if (signbit(area2)) { wind = -1.0f; }

  for (i = 0; i != n; i++) {
    uint8_t a = first_wp + i;
    uint8_t b = first_wp + ((i + 1) % n);
    float ex = waypoints[b].x - waypoints[a].x;
    float ey = waypoints[b].y - waypoints[a].y;
    float len = sqrtf(ex * ex + ey * ey);
    if (signbit(len - 0.001f)) { continue; }
    /* signed distance from this edge, positive towards the inside */
    float dist = wind * (ex * (y - waypoints[a].y) - ey * (x - waypoints[a].x)) / len;
    if (signbit(dist - margin)) { return false; }
  }
  return true;
}

/* Shoot ONLY inside the fire zone, and only with the wings near level.

   Position is the aircraft's, not the footprint's: a frame taken just inside
   the edge still images 13.4 m past it along track (26.8 m footprint at
   50 m), so the edges stay covered while nothing is shot from outside.

   The gate LOOKS AHEAD by `lookahead` seconds along the ground velocity:
   it runs at the navigation rate (20 Hz) while dc_periodic runs on its own
   at 10 Hz, so a shot can land up to one navigation period after the gate
   last saw the aircraft inside. Requiring the look-ahead point inside too
   closes the gate just before the edge by exactly that much track, at any
   edge angle. A fixed inward margin instead grows to margin/sin(angle) at
   an edge crossed obliquely, and clipped the tips of sharp corners. */
static inline void mission2_shoot_in_zone(float max_roll_deg, uint8_t first_wp, uint8_t n, float lookahead)
{
  /* [0].phi, not an arrow: a raw angle bracket in this header corrupts the
     generated flight_plan.xml, and Xml-light rejects it outright. */
  /* Roll no longer gates shots: shoot at any bank angle. */
  (void)max_roll_deg;
  bool shoot = true;
  float x = stateGetPositionEnu_f()[0].x;
  float y = stateGetPositionEnu_f()[0].y;
  float ax = x + stateGetSpeedEnu_f()[0].x * lookahead;
  float ay = y + stateGetSpeedEnu_f()[0].y * lookahead;
  if (!mission2_inside_zone(first_wp, n, x, y, 0.0f)) { shoot = false; }
  if (!mission2_inside_zone(first_wp, n, ax, ay, 0.0f)) { shoot = false; }
  if (shoot) {
    dc_autoshoot = DC_AUTOSHOOT_DISTANCE;
  } else {
    dc_autoshoot = DC_AUTOSHOOT_STOP;
  }
}
  </header>

